==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{ety|en|:af|over-|blow<t:to flower, bloom>|text=+|tree=1}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|~s|~ing|overblew|~n}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[cover]] with [[blossom]]s or [[flower]]s.

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|overblowen}}, equivalent to {{prefix|en|over|blow}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|~s|~ing|overblew|~n}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To blow over or across.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To blow away; [[dissipate]] by or as by wind.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[exaggerate]] the [[significance]] of something.
# {{lb|en|transitive|music}} To [[blow]] a [[wind instrument]] (typically a whistle, recorder or flute) hard to produce a higher [[pitch]] than usual.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1909|author=Leander Jan Bekker|title=Stokes' Encyclopedia of Music and Musicians
|passage=The upper octaves of the flute's compass are produced by '''overblowing'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|music}} Of a [[wind instrument]], to move from its lower to its higher register.
#: {{ux|en|The oboe '''overblows''' at the [[octave]]; the clarinet at the twelfth.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|nautical|archaic}} Of the [[wind#Noun|wind]]: to blow very hard, often resulting in ships unable to carry [[full sail#Noun|full sail]].
#* {{RQ:Swift Gulliver|part=II|page=151|passage=Finding it was like to '''overblow''', we took in our Sprit-ſail, and ſtood by to hand the Fore-ſail; but making foul Weather, we look'd the Guns were all faſt, and handed the Miſſen.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To blow over; pass over; pass away.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|2|2|text=But art thou not drown'd, Stephano? I hope now thou are / not drown'd. Is the storm '''overblown'''?}}

